course: dictionary
lesson: 3943
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Fullness is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it, which are its context. Ask yourself: what does this word mean in this sentence?
explain: «He knew it at last, in all its fullness ... that thing which the Rat had vainly tried to shield him from.» Here fullness means the whole, complete extent of the thing. The Rat tried to protect him from it, and it was so big and so real that it came out in full. Vainly means without success, so the Rat could not hide it.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «In all its whole extent» still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning. Keep this habit and you will understand many new words.
fact: Fullness is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say «the fullness», not «a fullness».
fact: «Fullness» can mean being full, as in «a feeling of fullness after dinner».
fact: «Fullness» can mean being complete or whole, as in «the fullness of the moon», which means the full moon.
fact: «Fullness» can mean being rich, deep or round, as in «the fullness of her voice».
fact: «In the fullness of time» is a formal phrase meaning in the end, when the right time comes.
exercise: «She felt a fullness after dinner, so she pushed her plate away.» What does fullness mean here? | Being very quick | Being cold and wet | *Being full, after eating | Being empty and hungry
exercise: «The fullness of the moon lit up the whole field.» What does fullness mean here? | Being rich in sound | Being small and thin | *Being complete or whole | Being hungry
exercise: «In the fullness of time, the seed grew into a tree.» What does the phrase «in the fullness of time» mean? | During dinner | *In the end, when the right time comes | In a rush, before anyone is ready | At the very start, straight away
test: «He spoke with the fullness of a deep, round voice.» What does fullness mean here? | Being empty and quiet | Being hungry | Being in a hurry | *Being rich, deep or round
test: «He knew it at last, in all its fullness.» What does «in all its fullness» mean? | A small part of it | *The whole, complete extent of it | Very quietly | Without any sound
exam: Which meaning of fullness is used in «the fullness of the moon»? | Being rich and deep | Being full after eating | *Being complete or whole | Being in the end, when the time is right
